  "articles": [
    {
      "sequence_id": 1,
      "text": "In Hindi, many verbs form their past tenses by using the root plus one of the four gender-number endings आ, ए, ई, ईं. For a certain class of verbs, however, the subject in the past tense is marked with the special postposition ‘ने’. This particle has no translatable meaning, but it disconnects the subject from the verb so that the verb remains in its neutral form. The verb therefore does not agree with the subject when ‘ने’ is used. Examples include: ‘मैंने कहा’ (I said), ‘आपने कहा’ (you said), ‘हमने कहा’ (we said) and ‘किसने कहा?’ (who said?). Other sentences illustrate that the verb form remains unchanged while the subject is marked by ‘ने’, such as ‘इन्होने पूछा’ (he asked), ‘उन्होंने बताया’ (he told), ‘जिसने लिखा’ (the person who wrote), ‘इसने सुना’ (he heard), ‘उसने पढ़ा’ (he read), and ‘सब लोगों ने खाया’ (everybody ate). In these examples the subject carries ‘ने’ and the verb keeps its neutral ending. This rule applies to many past-tense constructions where the subject is marked by ‘ने’ and does not agree with the verb."
    }
